    "lawCode": "Amd §§4-128, 4-132 & 8-302, El L",
    "lawSection": "",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:A1002\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the election law, in relation to a voter\nclaiming to live within an election district in which the voter seeks to\nvote being advised of the proper polling place and election district and\nbeing allowed to vote in said election district\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL: To enable voters to vote in the correct\npolling place by providing such items as maps, street finders, or other\ndescriptions of polling places are provided to poll-watchers. To require\na poll clerk or election inspector to consult a map, street finder or\nother description of all of the polling places in the election districts\nwithin the county in order to advise the voter of his or her correct\npolling place, so as to ensure that a vote cast by affidavit ballot is\ncast in the correct polling place and will, therefore, be counted.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS: Section one amends the section heading\nand subdivision 1 of section 4-128 of the election law, as amended by\nchapter 91 of the laws of 1992, by requiring the board of elections of\neach county to provide a sufficient number of maps, street finders or\nother descriptions of all the polling places and election districts\nwithin the county in which the polling place is located in addition to\nthe items that they are already required to provide. Such items will\nenable the election inspectors and poll clerks to determine the correct\nelection district and polling place for each street address within the\ncounty in which the polling place is located and to, in turn, direct\nvoters to that proper voting location.\n\nSection two amends the opening paragraph of section 4-132 of the\nelection law, as amended by chapter 164 of the laws of 1985, and adds a\nnew subdivision d which adds a sufficient number of maps, street finders\nor other descriptions of all polling places and election districts with-\nin the county in which the polling place is located to the list of\nthings to be supplied by the board of elections or the town clerk.\n\nSection three amends the opening paragraph of paragraph (e) of subdivi-\nsion 3 of section 8-302 of the election law, as amended by chapter 200\nof the laws of 1996, to require a poll clerk or election inspector to\nconsult a map, street finder or other description of all of the polling\nplaces and election districts within the county in which such election\ndistrict is located in order to advise the voter of his or her correct\npolling place and election district whenever a voter claims to live in\nthe election district in which he or she wishes to cast his or her\nballot and there is no record of such voter.\n\nSection four defines the effective date as immediately.\n\nJUSTIFICATION: The purpose of these amendments is to ensure that voters\nwho vote by affidavit ballot have a realistic chance to exercise the\nfranchise. A voter has the option of voting by affidavit ballot when the\ninspectors of election (hereinafter \"poll workers\") cannot find the\nvoter's registration in the records at the polling place on election\n\nday. As recently affirmed by the Court of Appeals in Matter of Panio v.\nSunderland, 4 NY3d 123, 791 NYS2d 57 (2005), affidavit ballots cast by\nvoters in the wrong polling place cannot be counted. In Matter of Panio,\nthe Court of Appeals rejected the claim that the failure to provide\nstreet finders for poll workers to consult before giving voters affida-\nvit ballots was a ministerial error by the Board of Elections. The\nproposed amendments are necessary to insure that the affidavit ballot\ndoes not become what one commentator has termed a \"placebo ballot,\"\ninstead of a fail-safe method by which duly registered voters may vote\non election day, as mandated by the Help America Vote Act (HAVA). (Ari\nWeisbard, \"Placebo Ballots Will 'Fail-Safe' Voting Fail?,\"\nwww.usa.org/pub295.cfm) The proposed amendments to Election Law §§ 4-128\nand 4-132 will insure that poll workers are supplied with county-wide\nstreet finders to enable them to determine whether voters are in the\ncorrect polling place on election day. In addition, the amendment to\nElection Law §8-302 will mandate that poll workers have a duty to check\nthe street finder prior to providing a voter with an affidavit ballot.\n\nAffidavit ballots are utilized by voters whose names do not appear in\nthe registration records. A voter's name may not appear in the registra-\ntion records because the registration poll record may be lost or\nmisplaced, the voter may have been placed on \"inactive\" status pursuant\nto Election Law §5-213, or the voter may have moved within the county\nand remain eligible to vote by affidavit ballot at the polling place for\nthe voter's new residence (while his name remains in the poll book for\nhis prior address).\n\nThe Election Law does not make it easy for voters to determine where to\nvote. Voters are sent a \"check\" card, pursuant to Election Law\n§4-117(1). This card is used to verify that voters are still residing at\nthe address at which they registered to vote, as well as to advise\nvoters of a change in the location of their polling place. However, the\ncard must be sent more than two months before election day and it is not\nsent to voters whose status is \"inactive.\"  Id. In addition, because the\ncheck card is not forwardable, it is not received by any voter who has\nmoved.  Id. So, the two largest classes of voters for whom affidavit\nballots were created -- inactive voters and voters who have moved --\nwill never receive the cards advising them where to vote, and those\nvoters who do receive them, will get them at some time prior to Labor\nDay for an election to take place in November.\n\nFor a voter who has never received notification of where to vote, or did\nso months before election day, it is not a simple process to go to the\nright polling place. And in the absence of a comprehensive street\nfinder, a poll worker lacks the tools to direct a voter who has ended up\nin the wrong place on election day.\n\nThe poll workers provide the affidavit ballot to the voter. Prior to\nproviding it to a voter whose name does not appear in the registration\nrecords for that polling place, Election Law §8-302(1) currently\nprovides that the poll workers have the responsibility of \"verifying the\nright of persons to vote.\" However, the Election Law does not require\n\nthat the poll workers ascertain whether the voter is in the polling\nplace assigned for his or her address prior to giving the voter an affi-\ndavit ballot. And although the Board of Elections is required to provide\npoll workers with \"a map or certified description of the election\ndistrict and adjoining election districts,\" Election Law §5-502(1), that\nis insufficient to direct a voter to the correct polling place if the\nvoter resides elsewhere in the county.\n\nIn Matter of Panio, supra, 457 affidavit ballots cast by duly registered\nvoters who were in the wrong polling place were rejected in a race that\nwas decided by 18 votes. The evidence presented at the trial showed that\nin one municipality the poll workers received at most only a few pages\nof street finders, rather than a comprehensive list, and that some poll\nworkers received no street finders at all (Trial Transcript, pp. 1322,\n231-232). As a result, if the poll workers could not find a voter's name\nin their poll book, they could not determine whether the voter was in\nthe right polling place. These voters were effectively disenfranchised.\n\nAn affidavit ballot is not a realistic alternative if it has no chance\nof being counted because the voter casts it in the wrong polling place.\nIt is incumbent upon the Legislature to insure that poll workers have\nstreet finders to enable them to direct voters to the correct place and\nto mandate that poll workers consult the street finder before supplying\na voter with an affidavit ballot.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:  7256, 2008 and 2007 referred to election\nlaw. Same as S.6991 (Stewart-Cousins), 2008 referred to election.\nA9313-A, 2006 advanced to third reading.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATION: None to the state.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE: Immediately.\n\n",
